<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Displacement and distance travelled</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>A goods lift in a building in Nairobi starts from rest at \(t = 0\). Taking upwards as positive, its velocity after \(t\) seconds is \(v = {@vt@}\) m/s. Give exact values.</p><p>(a) When, after \(t = 0\), is the lift at rest?</p><div>\(t =\) [[input:ans1]] s [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find its displacement from \(t = 0\) to \(t = {@Tend@}\).</p><div>Displacement \(=\) [[input:ans2]] m [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the total distance it travels from \(t = 0\) to \(t = {@Tend@}\).</p><div>Distance \(=\) [[input:ans3]] m [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Displacement is the single integral of \(v\): motion downwards counts as negative. The distance travelled is the total area between the \(v\)-\(t\) graph and the \(t\)-axis, so split at the times of rest and add the sizes.</p><p>(a) \(v = {@mm@}t({@rr@} - t) = 0\) when \(t = 0\) or \(t = {@rr@}\). The lift goes up until \(t = {@rr@}\), then comes down.</p><p>(b) \(\displaystyle\int_0^{ {@Tend@} } v\,dt = \left[{@St@}\right]_0^{ {@Tend@} } = {@exd(Dv)@}\) m.</p><p>(c) Up: \(\displaystyle\int_0^{ {@rr@} } v\,dt = {@exd(A1)@}\) m. Down: \(\displaystyle\int_{ {@rr@} }^{ {@Tend@} } v\,dt = {@exd(-A2)@}\), so \({@exd(A2)@}\) m. Distance \(= {@exd(A1)@} + {@exd(A2)@} = {@exd(dist)@}\) m.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[/* exd: shortest exact decimal display of a terminating value (up to 3 d.p.) */
exd(v) := if integerp(v) then v else if integerp(10*v) then dispdp(float(v),1) else if integerp(100*v) then dispdp(float(v),2) else dispdp(float(v),3);
/* Design: areas above and below the axis of a v-t graph, by calculus. [m, r, T]: v = m t (r - t). Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Mechanics 1, chapter 1, section C, C2. */
L: [[3/20,8,11],[3/5,4,5],[3/20,6,7],[1/10,12,15],[3/20,10,12],[3/20,10,14],[3/10,6,8],[3/32,12,14],[3/20,8,10],[3/20,8,9]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
mm: L[ix][1]; rr: L[ix][2]; Tend: L[ix][3];
vt: mm*rr*t-mm*t^2;
St: integrate(vt, t);
Dv: subst(t=Tend, St);
A1: subst(t=rr, St);
A2: A1-Dv;
dist: A1+A2;]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
